Goals
  * Setup local DNS filtering using Pi-hole.
  * Understand DNS behavior and its role in network security within a live environment.
  * Reduce tracking and advertisements across devices on my home internet. 

Tools Used
  * Raspberry Pi 5
  * Raspberry Pi OS
  * Pi-hole
  * SSH from MacOS
  * Github DNS blocklist
  * Google Nest Wifi

What I Did
  * Installed and imaged the Pi OS onto the Raspberry Pi.
  * Connected the device to my home internet via ethernet. 
  * Used SSH to remotely run commands and manage the system. 
  * Installed and configured Pi-hole.
  * Imported blocklist from github.
  * Configured my network to use the Pi-hole IP address as the DNS server. 
  * Monitored live traffic through the Pi-hole dashboard. 

Why
  * Used Raspberry Pi becuase it is a low power, always on device. 
  * Managed the system over SSH to practice Linux adminstration,
  * Implemented blocklist to increase DNS filtering.
  * Reduce spam advertisements and analtyic tracking across home devices. 
  * Gain visibiilty into DNS traffic patterns and identify anomalies 

Problems
  * All DNS request were coming from a single IP address. 
      - While monitoring the Pi-hole dashboard, all DNS queries were shown coming from one IP, and hitting the request limit very fast. 
       After some troubleshooting, I determined that my router was forwarding DNS request insteal of allowing devices to communicate directly with my Pi-hole.
       Because my router manages DHCP and DNS behabior internally, I could not configure DHCP to distributre Pi-hole as the default DNS server.
       To restore per device visibility, I manually configured the Pi-hole IP address as the DNS server on my devices. 
       This enabled Pi-hole to log request by client, and no more timeout issues. 
Learned
  * How DNS resolution works and how traffic flows through a local network. 
  * How DNS filtering can be a useful security tool.
  * How to manage Linux devices remotely using SSH.
  * The volume of DNS request that are generated daily. 
